  {
    "id": "weight_balance", "title": "Weight & Balance", "track": "general", "icon": "&#x2696;",
    "sections": [
      {"heading": "Why It Matters", "body": "An aircraft loaded outside CG limits may be uncontrollable. The mechanic maintains the <b>weight and balance record</b> and reweighs after major repairs/alterations. Every equipment change updates the record."},
      {"heading": "Key Terms", "body": "<b>Datum</b> - reference plane. <b>Arm</b> - distance from datum. <b>Moment</b> = weight x arm. <b>CG</b> = total moment / total weight. <b>Empty weight</b>, <b>useful load</b>, <b>MTOW</b> (max takeoff weight). Forward/aft CG limits from TCDS."},
      {"heading": "Weighing Procedure", "body": "Level the aircraft, use calibrated scales at each wheel/jack point. Subtract tare. Drainable fuel out (or note), full oil. Compute empty weight and empty-weight CG. Record in the aircraft records."},
      {"heading": "Adverse Loading & Ballast", "body": "Check most-forward and most-aft load cases stay within envelope. <b>Ballast</b> (permanent or temporary) corrects out-of-limit CG - must be placarded and secured. Equipment removal can shift CG dangerously."}
    ,
      {"heading": "Worked CG Example", "body": "Empty weight 1400 lb at arm 40 in gives moment 56,000 lb-in. Add pilot 170 lb at arm 37 = 6,290 lb-in. Add fuel 180 lb at arm 48 = 8,640 lb-in. Total weight 1750 lb, total moment 70,930 lb-in. <b>CG = 70,930 / 1750 = 40.5 in</b> - compare against the TCDS forward/aft limits to confirm the loading is legal before flight."}, {"heading": "Empty Weight CG Range and Equipment Changes", "body": "Every aircraft has an approved empty weight center of gravity (CG) range specified in its weight and balance data, and any equipment addition, removal, or relocation (avionics upgrades, seat changes, interior modifications) requires a new weight and balance calculation to confirm the aircraft remains within approved limits. Cumulative minor equipment changes over an aircraft's life can gradually shift the empty weight CG outside the approved envelope even though no single change seemed significant, which is why a full reweigh is periodically required by many manufacturers and after any major alteration. Technicians must update the permanent weight and balance record with the new empty weight, CG location, and a list of installed equipment after every applicable change."}, {"heading": "Equipment List and Weight & Balance Recordkeeping", "body": "Every aircraft has an approved Equipment List that identifies installed equipment, its weight, and its arm (location relative to the datum). When equipment is added, removed, or relocated, mechanics must update both the Equipment List and the Weight and Balance record to reflect the new empty weight and empty weight CG. Failure to keep these records current can result in a pilot performing loading calculations against inaccurate data, potentially leading to an out-of-limits CG in flight. Major changes affecting weight and balance, such as installing a new avionics package or interior modification, typically require a new weighing or a calculated revision using STC or approved data, along with appropriate logbook entries. Mechanics are responsible for ensuring that any weight and balance revision is signed, dated, and traceable to the maintenance action that caused the change."}, {"heading": "Datum Selection and Moment Arm Sign Conventions", "body": "Weight and balance calculations depend on a defined datum, a reference plane or point from which all arm measurements are taken, and this datum can be located at various positions such as the aircraft's nose, a firewall, or an arbitrary point specified by the manufacturer, with the specific location documented in the aircraft's weight and balance data. Arms measured forward or aft of the datum use sign conventions (typically negative for forward of datum, positive for aft, though this varies by manufacturer and must be verified against the specific aircraft's documentation) that must be applied consistently throughout a calculation, since a sign error in even one component's arm can produce a significantly incorrect center of gravity result. When equipment is relocated during maintenance, its new arm relative to the established datum must be measured or calculated accurately, and mechanics must never assume a component's arm without verification, since visually estimating position relative to the datum introduces error that compounds when combined with other components in a full weight and balance calculation. Understanding datum location and sign convention is foundational to correctly interpreting and performing any weight and balance calculation, and confusion on this point is a common source of calculation errors among students first learning the topic."},
    {"heading": "Ballast Installation Requirements and Placarding", "body": "When an aircraft's CG falls outside limits for a particular loading condition, permanent or removable ballast may be installed to shift the CG back into range. Permanent ballast is typically fixed metal weight securely attached to structure at an approved station, documented on the equipment list and weight and balance record, and is not intended for removal during normal operations. Removable ballast (such as sandbags or weight kits in a baggage compartment) must be secured against shifting in flight and in turbulence, since unsecured ballast becomes a hazardous projectile and a CG-shifting risk. Any ballast installation requires a placard at the ballast location stating the ballast weight and, where applicable, a warning against removal or relocation, plus a corresponding weight and balance revision showing the new empty weight and CG. Ballast should never be considered a substitute for reweighing when an aircraft's actual CG range does not match its equipment configuration; it is used specifically to correct a documented, calculated CG deficiency. Technicians must also verify that ballast placement does not exceed local structural load limits and that securing hardware (straps, brackets, bolts) meets the applicable load factor requirements for the installation."},
    {"heading": "Weight and Balance Impact of Repair Materials and Added Equipment", "body": "Every maintenance action that adds, removes, or relocates material on an aircraft has a potential weight and balance impact, and technicians must evaluate whether the change is significant enough to require updating the aircraft's weight and balance records, since accumulated undocumented changes can gradually shift the aircraft's empty weight and empty weight center of gravity outside its certificated limits without any single change appearing significant in isolation. Structural repairs that add doubler plates, patches, or reinforcing material add weight at the specific station of the repair, and while a single small doubler may add only a fraction of a pound, multiple repairs across the aircraft or a single large repair in a location far from the center of gravity can produce a measurable CG shift that affects the valid loading envelope the operator depends on. Added equipment such as supplemental oxygen equipment, emergency locator transmitter upgrades, avionics additions, or interior modifications must be entered in the equipment list with their actual weight and moment arm, and the aircraft's new empty weight and empty weight CG must be calculated and recorded on a revised weight and balance report that supersedes the previous one. Removed equipment presents the equal and opposite consideration, since removing original equipment reduces weight and changes CG, and the technician must verify that removing the item does not leave the aircraft's empty weight CG outside the allowable range for the remaining loading configurations, since an aircraft that was certified with specific ballast arrangements may not fly safely with that weight removed. Large structural repairs or complete section replacements, such as a fuselage skin panel replacement with a heavier repair skin or a replacement control surface with slightly different weight, warrant particular attention since these involve significant areas and any weight difference per square foot multiplies across the replaced area."},
    {"heading": "Fuel Burn Effects on Center of Gravity During Flight Operations", "body": "As fuel is consumed during flight, the aircraft's center of gravity shifts depending on the location of the fuel tanks relative to the aircraft's datum and the sequence in which fuel is burned from multiple tanks. Aircraft with wing-mounted fuel tanks located near the aircraft's CG generally experience minimal CG shift as fuel burns, while aircraft with fuel tanks located significantly forward or aft of the CG, or with asymmetric fuel burn sequencing, can experience a more significant CG shift over the course of a flight. Weight and balance calculations for flight planning must account for this shift by verifying that the CG remains within approved limits not only at takeoff but throughout the flight as fuel is consumed, particularly checking the most aft or most forward CG condition depending on the fuel system's burn sequence and tank locations. Aircraft with tanks that must be burned in a specific sequence for CG management reasons will have that sequence specified in the flight manual, and deviating from the specified fuel burn sequence can result in the aircraft exceeding CG limits even though the total fuel quantity remains within limits."}],
    "quiz": [ {"q": "Why must the sign convention for arms (forward vs. aft of datum) be applied consistently throughout a weight and balance calculation?", "choices": ["Sign convention has no effect on the final CG calculation", "A sign error in even one component's arm can produce a significantly incorrect center of gravity result", "Signs are only relevant for aft-mounted equipment", "All arms are always treated as positive regardless of position"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "When equipment is permanently added to an aircraft, what records must be updated to remain accurate?", "choices": ["Only the pilot's operating handbook", "The Equipment List and the Weight and Balance record", "Only the aircraft registration", "Nothing, since minor equipment changes are ignored"], "answer": 1}, {"q": "Why might a full aircraft reweigh be required periodically even without a major single alteration?", "choices": ["Only pilots request it for fun", "Cumulative minor equipment changes can gradually shift the empty weight CG outside approved limits", "Weight and balance never changes", "Fuel weight fully compensates for any change"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Small equipment changes over time can accumulate and shift the empty weight CG outside the approved envelope, which is why periodic reweighs and updates to records are required."},
      {"q": "Moment equals:", "choices": ["Weight / arm","Weight x arm","Arm / weight","Weight + arm"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Moment = weight x arm (a torque about the datum)."},
      {"q": "CG is calculated as:", "choices": ["Total weight / total moment","Total moment / total weight","Arm x datum","Weight x MTOW"], "answer": 1, "explain": "CG = total moment divided by total weight."},
      {"q": "The datum is:", "choices": ["The heaviest point","A reference plane for measurements","The CG","The tail"], "answer": 1, "explain": "The datum is a reference plane from which all arms are measured."},
      {"q": "Ballast must be:", "choices": ["Loose in the cabin","Placarded and secured","Painted red","Removed for flight"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Permanent/temporary ballast must be secured and placarded per approved data."},
      {"q": "Empty weight is 1400 lb at arm 40 in. What is the moment?", "choices": ["35 lb-in","1440 lb-in","56,000 lb-in","1360 lb-in"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Moment = weight x arm = 1400 x 40 = 56,000 lb-in."},
      {"q": "When must W&B be redone?", "choices": ["Every flight","After major repair/alteration or equipment change","Only at annual","Never"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Reweigh or recompute after major repairs, alterations, or equipment changes."},
    {"q": "What must accompany any ballast installation used to bring an aircraft's CG into limits?", "choices": ["Nothing, ballast requires no documentation", "A placard stating ballast weight and location, plus a weight and balance record revision", "Only a verbal notation to the next pilot", "Removal of the aircraft's equipment list"], "answer": 1},
    {"q": "Why must accumulated small structural repairs be tracked for weight and balance impact even when each individual repair seems insignificant?", "choices": ["Multiple small additions can gradually shift empty weight CG outside certificated limits without any single change appearing significant enough to require attention", "Weight and balance records only need to be updated when an aircraft undergoes a major alteration, never for repairs", "Small repairs always reduce aircraft weight and improve CG position", "Weight and balance is only relevant at initial aircraft certification and never changes during service"], "answer": 0},
    {"q": "Why must weight and balance calculations account for fuel burn sequence rather than just total fuel quantity?", "choices": ["Fuel burn sequence has no effect on CG", "CG can shift during flight as fuel burns from different tank locations, and improper sequencing can move CG outside limits even with total fuel within limits", "Only takeoff CG matters, not in-flight CG", "Fuel burn sequence only affects fuel efficiency, not CG"], "answer": 1}
    ]
  }
